---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RecordDemo from '../components/RecordDemo.astro';
import Releases from '../components/Releases.astro';
import releases from '../data/releases.json';
import { REPO, WIKI, ISSUES, EMAIL, PACKAGES, wiki } from '../data/links';

const mvx = releases.packages.find((p) => p.id === 'mvx');
const latest = mvx?.stable;
const download = latest ? latest.url : `${REPO}/releases/latest`;

// The flat 1M prime sieve over five seconds, as a share of a C sieve run on
// the same machine.  Measured September 2026, except jBASE (August 2026).
const bench = [
  { name: 'mvx', passes: 14011, c: 14387, note: 'LLVM, native code' },
  { name: 'jBASE 6.2.1 with -O4', passes: 216, c: 13708, note: 'compiled through C' },
  { name: 'UniData 8.3', passes: 68, c: 14247, note: 'interpreted' },
  { name: 'OpenQM (ScarletDME)', passes: 46, c: 14152, note: 'interpreted' },
].map((b) => ({ ...b, pct: (b.passes / b.c) * 100 }));

const drivers = [
  { name: 'SQLite', text: 'The default for a new account. One file, nothing to run.' },
  { name: 'PostgreSQL', text: 'Records as jsonb, typed columns from your dictionaries, native indexes, and record locks shared across processes.' },
  { name: 'MongoDB', text: 'A document per record, mapped fields beside it, and associations as embedded arrays.' },
  { name: 'MySQL and MariaDB', text: 'The same document shape, on the database many shops already run.' },
  { name: 'LMDB', text: 'Embedded and very fast, for a single host.' },
  { name: 'mvx-lmdbd', text: 'LMDB behind a network daemon, so many hosts share one set of files.' },
  { name: 'Directory files', text: 'Each record is a plain OS file, so source code sits where git and your editor can see it.' },
];

const docs = [
  { slug: 'getting-started', title: 'Getting started', text: 'Build, install and open your first account.' },
  { slug: 'language', title: 'The language', text: 'MVX BASIC, statements and intrinsic functions.' },
  { slug: 'tcl', title: 'TCL', text: 'The command shell and its verbs.' },
  { slug: 'files', title: 'Files and dictionaries', text: 'Records, dictionaries, queries, indexes, and mapping to SQL and JSON.' },
  { slug: 'deployment', title: 'Deployment', text: 'Storage drivers, named connections, PostgreSQL and MongoDB.' },
  { slug: 'containers', title: 'Containers', text: 'The published images and how to run them.' },
  { slug: 'packages', title: 'Packages', text: 'Installing and publishing with MVPKG.' },
  { slug: 'git', title: 'Version control', text: 'Hash-file records under git.' },
];
---
<Base
  title="mvx: Pick BASIC, compiled"
  description="mvx is an open-source MultiValue system: Pick BASIC compiled to native code with LLVM, built to run in containers, with your data on PostgreSQL, MongoDB, SQLite or LMDB."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <h1>Pick BASIC,<br />compiled to native&nbsp;code.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      mvx is a MultiValue system built with today's tools. An LLVM compiler turns your BASIC into real
      machine code with a real debugger. It ships as containers, and it keeps your data in PostgreSQL,
      MongoDB or a local store, whichever your team already runs.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={download} data-mvx-version>Download mvx {latest?.version}</a>
      <a class="btn btn-plain" href={WIKI}>Read the documentation</a>
      <a class="btn btn-plain" href={REPO}>View the source</a>
    </div>
    <div class="try">
      <p>Or try it now, with nothing to install but Docker:</p>
      <pre><code>docker run --rm -it ghcr.io/mvx-lang/mvx-demo</code></pre>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="data-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="intro">
        <h2 id="data-h">Your records, in the database you already run.</h2>
        <p>
          Every file in an account is bound to a storage driver, and one account can mix them. The
          dictionary you already have becomes the schema. Programs keep reading and writing records,
          while reporting tools read the same live data as rows or documents. There is no export and
          no nightly copy.
        </p>
      </div>
      <RecordDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap split" aria-labelledby="store-h">
    <div class="intro">
      <h2 id="store-h">Three commands to put a file on PostgreSQL.</h2>
      <p>
        Define a connection once and bind files to it by name. The host and password live outside
        the account, so the account can go under version control and a container can supply its
        target through environment variables.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>CREATE-MAP</code> names the fields you want as columns. From then on every
        <code>WRITE</code> keeps them up to date, changing only what moved.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="stack">
      <figure>
        <figcaption>At the mvx prompt</figcaption>
        <pre><code>&gt; SET-CONNECTION pgmain driver=postgres address=db:5432 dbname=mvx user=app namespace=sales
&gt; CREATE-FILE ORDERS USING @pgmain
&gt; CREATE-MAP ORDERS CUSTOMER PRODUCT QTY PRICE</code></pre>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>In psql, before any mapping: attributes by number</figcaption>
        <pre><code>SELECT id, doc-&gt;&gt;'1' AS customer, doc-&gt;'2' AS products
  FROM sales."ORDERS";

 id     | customer  | products
--------+-----------+----------------------
 10042  | Acme Corp | ["Widget", "Gadget"]</code></pre>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>In psql, after mapping: names from the dictionary</figcaption>
        <pre><code>SELECT o."CUSTOMER", sum(i."QTY"::numeric * i."PRICE") AS total
  FROM sales."ORDERS" o
  JOIN sales."ORDERS_ORDERITEMS" i USING (id)
 GROUP BY o."CUSTOMER";

 CUSTOMER  | total
-----------+-------
 Acme Corp | 24.48</code></pre>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <figcaption>In mongosh, the same file bound to MongoDB</figcaption>
        <pre><code>use sales
db.ORDERS.aggregate([
  {'{'} $unwind: '$ORDERITEMS' {'}'},
  {'{'} $group: {'{'}
      _id: '$CUSTOMER',
      total: {'{'} $sum: {'{'} $multiply: [
        {'{'} $toInt: '$ORDERITEMS.QTY' {'}'}, '$ORDERITEMS.PRICE' ] {'}'} {'}'}
  {'}'} {'}'}
])

[ {'{'} _id: 'Acme Corp', total: 24.48 {'}'} ]</code></pre>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="drv-h">
    <h2 id="drv-h">Pick a backend per file.</h2>
    <dl class="drivers">
      {drivers.map((d) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{d.name}</dt>
          <dd>{d.text}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p class="more">
      Queries are pushed down to the database where it can answer them, so a <code>LIST</code> with a
      <code>WITH</code> clause returns only the matching records rather than the whole table.
      <a href={wiki('deployment')}>How the drivers work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap split" aria-labelledby="native-h">
    <div class="intro">
      <h2 id="native-h">Compiled, not interpreted.</h2>
      <p>
        mvx compiles BASIC straight to machine code through LLVM, the compiler toolkit behind clang
        and Swift. It does not translate to C first. Numeric variables become real integers and
        doubles, so arithmetic runs close to the speed of C.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also designed for today's hardware. The classic MultiValue systems were built when
        memory was measured in megabytes, and saved every byte. mvx trades some of today's gigabytes
        for speed: it keeps an index into each dynamic array, so reading or writing any element takes
        a few nanoseconds, however long the array is.
      </p>
      <p>
        The compiler also writes standard debug information mapped to your BASIC line numbers. gdb
        and lldb step through the program you wrote, and profilers such as perf and Instruments
        report time against your own source lines.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="bench">
      <figcaption>
        A prime sieve written in Pick BASIC, run for five seconds, as a share of a C version on the
        same machine.
      </figcaption>
      <ul>
        {bench.map((b) => (
          <li>
            <span class="b-name">{b.name} <span class="b-note">{b.note}</span></span>
            <span class="b-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span style={`--w:${Math.min(Math.max(b.pct, 0.6), 100).toFixed(2)}%`}></span></span>
            <span class="b-val">{b.pct < 10 ? b.pct.toFixed(1) : Math.round(b.pct)}% of C</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="b-src">
        Each system is measured against C on its own machine, in September 2026 (jBASE in August).
        <a href="/history/#speed">How these were measured</a>.
      </p>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="ctr-h">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="intro">
        <h2 id="ctr-h">Made to run in containers.</h2>
        <p>
          A session is an ordinary process, and a file can live on another machine. So an account can
          keep its programs local while its data sits on a shared database, and you add capacity by
          adding containers instead of buying a bigger server.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>
          <strong>Published images</strong> for <code>linux/amd64</code> and <code>linux/arm64</code>:
          <code>ghcr.io/mvx-lang/mvx</code>, <code>mvx-lmdbd</code> and <code>mvx-demo</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Relocatable tarballs</strong> that find their own runtime and drivers. No environment
          variables to set.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connections from the environment.</strong> Set <code>MVXCONN_SALESDB_ADDRESS</code>
          and the account follows, with no file to edit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Operations you already know.</strong> Backups, replication and monitoring are handled
          by PostgreSQL or MongoDB, with the tools your team uses today.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="sys-h">
    <h2 id="sys-h">A complete MultiValue system.</h2>
    <dl class="system">
      <div><dt>TCL shell</dt><dd>The classic command line, with its verbs written in BASIC and cataloged like any program.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Dictionaries and queries</dt><dd><code>LIST</code>, <code>SELECT</code> and <code>SORT</code> driven by your dictionaries, with conversions, formats and select lists.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Secondary indexes</dt><dd>Built on the backend itself where it has them.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Packages</dt><dd><code>MVPKG</code> installs libraries from <a href={PACKAGES}>packages.mvx-lang.org</a>, with dependencies resolved for you.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Version control</dt><dd>Records, dictionaries and VOC under git, from the <code>GIT</code> verb or the shell.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Permissions in the runtime</dt><dd>Restricted, developer and unrestricted sessions, enforced where a program cannot get around them.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="releases" aria-labelledby="rel-h">
    <h2 id="rel-h">Latest release</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      Relocatable builds for Linux. Installing mvx also installs MVPKG and git, and asks which
      version of each you want. Other packages are on <a href={PACKAGES}>packages.mvx-lang.org</a>.
    </p>
    <Releases />
    <pre class="install"><code><span data-mvx-version>tar xzf mvx-lang-{latest?.version}-linux-amd64.tar.gz</span>     # or -arm64
./mvx-lang/bin/mvx -a myaccount</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" aria-labelledby="doc-h">
    <h2 id="doc-h">Documentation</h2>
    <ul class="docs">
      {docs.map((d) => (
        <li>
          <a href={wiki(d.slug)}>{d.title}</a>
          <span>{d.text}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap port" aria-labelledby="port-h">
    <h2 id="port-h">Have a Pick application?</h2>
    <p>
      mvx gets better by running real software. If you have a UniVerse, UniData, jBASE, D3 or Reality
      application, or a framework such as SB+ or CueBic, that you would let me test against, I would
      like to hear from you. I'm happy to sign an NDA.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`mailto:${EMAIL}?subject=Porting%20to%20mvx`}>Email {EMAIL}</a>
      <a class="btn btn-plain" href={ISSUES}>Open an issue</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem); }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .intro p { color: var(--ink); }
  .intro { margin-bottom: 2rem; }

  .hero { padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 7rem) clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem); }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 14ch; }
  .lede { margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 1.6vw, 1.3rem); line-height: 1.55; max-width: 58ch; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .try { margin-top: 2.25rem; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem 1rem; }
  .try p { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .try pre { padding: 0.55rem 0.85rem; max-width: 100%; }

  .band { background: var(--band); border-block: 1px solid var(--rule); }

  .split { display: grid; gap: 2rem 4rem; align-items: start; }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr); }
    .split .intro { position: sticky; top: 5.5rem; margin-bottom: 0; }
  }

  .stack { display: grid; gap: 1.5rem; min-width: 0; }
  .stack figure, .bench { margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .stack figcaption { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-2); margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }

  .drivers, .system {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 17rem), 1fr));
    gap: 0;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .drivers > div, .system > div { padding: 1rem 1.5rem 1.1rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  dt { font: 600 1.05rem/1.3 var(--display); }
  dd { margin: 0.3rem 0 0; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 0.97rem; }
  .more { margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .bench figcaption { font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--ink-2); margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 50ch; }
  .bench ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
  .bench li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    grid-template-areas: 'name val' 'bar bar';
    gap: 0.35rem 1rem;
    align-items: baseline;
  }
  .b-name { grid-area: name; font-weight: 600; }
  .b-note { font-weight: 400; color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-left: 0.35rem; }
  .b-val { grid-area: val; font: 600 0.95rem/1 var(--mono); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .b-bar { grid-area: bar; height: 0.9rem; background: var(--paper-2); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 3px; overflow: hidden; }
  .b-bar > span { display: block; height: 100%; width: var(--w); background: var(--ink-2); }
  .bench li:first-child .b-bar > span { background: var(--accent); }
  .b-src { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--ink-2); }

  .facts { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1.25rem; }
  .facts li { padding-left: 1.1rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); }
  .facts strong { display: block; font-family: var(--display); font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.15rem; }

  .sub { color: var(--ink-2); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .install { margin-top: 1.5rem; }

  .docs {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr));
    gap: 0.25rem 2.5rem;
  }
  .docs li { padding-block: 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .docs a { font: 600 1.1rem/1.3 var(--display); display: block; }
  .docs span { color: var(--ink-2); font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .port { padding-bottom: 0; }
  .port p { font-size: 1.1rem; }
</style>
